//! image/selection_summary.rye -- read a SELECTION as a small table: JOIN each selected object's scene-manifest facts (area, holes)
//! over the SUBSET a selector named, and answer the questions a surface asks the moment a keeper has a selection in hand -- how much
//! is selected, which selected object is largest, which selected objects are holed, and the selection sorted by size. Exact integers,
//! NO float and NO tolerance.
//!
//! Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- the selection ladder now runs four rungs deep: image/hit_test.rye names the ONE object under a
//! POINT, image/region_select.rye names EVERY object a RECTANGLE covers, image/lasso_select.rye names EVERY object a POLYGON covers.
//! Each returns the same thing -- an ASCENDING SET of dense labels, the `[]u32` a surface holds after a tap, a drag, or a loop. This
//! rung is what a surface DOES with that set: it joins image/scene_manifest.rye's per-object rows over just the selected labels and
//! reads the selection as a whole. It composes over ANY selector, since all three speak the same label-set language.
//!
//! The DECISIVE crux: a selection is a SUBSET, so a read over the selection is strictly sharper than the same read over the whole
//! scene -- the scene's largest object, and the scene's largest holed object, can BOTH lie outside the selection. Three facts on
//! metal say it, over a scene of a big solid block (area 25, no hole), a small ring (area 8, holed), a large ring (area 16, holed),
//! and a small solid block (area 4, no hole):
//!
//!   THE SUBSET MOVES THE ANSWER -- for the selection {small ring, small block}, `largest_in_selection` names the SMALL RING (area
//!   8), though the scene's largest object is the solid block (area 25, unselected); `largest_holed_in_selection` names the SMALL
//!   RING too, though the scene's `largest_holed` is the LARGE RING (area 16, unselected). Neither whole-scene read lands where the
//!   selection read does -- the join is over the subset, not the scene.
//!
//!   THE FILTER IS SHARPER THAN THE SCENE'S -- that same selection holds one holed object (`holed_in_selection_count` reads 1), while
//!   the scene holds two; a holed object outside the selection does not count. And a NON-EMPTY selection of only unholed objects
//!   refuses `NoHoledInSelection` -- the refusal is about holes, not emptiness, exactly as image/scene_manifest.rye's `largest_holed`.
//!
//!   THE SORT REVERSES THE LABEL ORDER -- for the selection {small ring, large ring} (labels ascending 2, 3), `sort_selection_by_area`
//!   returns the LARGE RING FIRST (labels 3, 2), since area descending disagrees with label ascending. A selection arrives sorted by
//!   label; a keeper wants it sorted by size, and only the join over the manifest can reorder it.
//!
//! Compose not braid. This rung OWNS only the reads over a selection -- the joins, the filter, the sort. image/scene_manifest.rye
//! owns the per-object rows (`ObjectEntry`, `Manifest`, `object_of`, `total_area`) every read looks up; the selectors own the label
//! set. No plane of its own, no pixel written, no fact re-derived -- a reading over a reading.
//!
//! Everything is bounded before a byte: a valid selection names at most one label per component, so its length is bounded by the
//! component count, itself under image/label.rye's `label_max_pixels`; every read runs the selection length times, each a single
//! manifest lookup; the sort is a bounded insertion sort over the copied labels, at most length-squared comparisons under that same
//! ceiling. A selection that names background (0), a label past the component count, or labels out of strictly-ascending order
//! refuses `BadSelection`, having read nothing -- the contract every selector in the ladder keeps, checked at the edge. An empty
//! selection is valid: `total_selected_area` is zero, `largest_in_selection` refuses `EmptySelection` by name. No network, no key, no
//! funds -- the selection read a surface names the day a keeper has objects chosen and asks what they add up to, on our own open
//! geometry.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run image/selection_summary.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const label = @import("label.rye");
const qoi = @import("qoi.rye");
const scene_manifest = @import("scene_manifest.rye");

pub const selection_summary_version = "20260818.232731";

pub const SelectionSummaryError = error{
    BadSelection,
    EmptySelection,
    NoHoledInSelection,
};

/// Check a selection is a valid label set -- every entry a real dense label (1..components), the entries strictly ascending (hence
/// distinct), and no more entries than components. This is the contract every selector in the ladder keeps; a read enforces it at the
/// edge and refuses `BadSelection` before touching a fact, so no read ever indexes a phantom object. Reads nothing on refusal.
fn validate_selection(manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, selection: []const u32) SelectionSummaryError!void {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, manifest.components));
    // invariant: the component count stays under image/label.rye's pixel ceiling, so the check loop is bounded.
    assert(manifest.components <= label.label_max_pixels);

    // a valid selection names at most one label per component -- a longer slice cannot be strictly ascending within the dense range,
    // so bound the loop here and refuse before reading.
    if (selection.len > @as(usize, manifest.components)) return error.BadSelection;

    var prev: u32 = 0;
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < selection.len) : (i += 1) {
        const lbl = selection[i];
        // a selection names foreground labels only, never background and never past the component count.
        if (lbl == 0 or lbl > manifest.components) return error.BadSelection;
        // strictly ascending -- which also guarantees distinct, since equal or descending both fail here.
        if (lbl <= prev) return error.BadSelection;
        prev = lbl;
    }
}

/// The TOTAL foreground area of the selection -- the sum of every selected object's area. A valid empty selection reads zero. Refuses
/// `BadSelection` for a malformed label set.
pub fn total_selected_area(manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, selection: []const u32) !u64 {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, manifest.components));
    try validate_selection(manifest, selection);

    var sum: u64 = 0;
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < selection.len) : (i += 1) {
        const entry = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, selection[i]);
        sum += @as(u64, entry.area);
    }
    // invariant: a subset's area never exceeds the whole scene's total area -- a selection cannot hold more than the scene does.
    assert(sum <= scene_manifest.total_area(manifest));
    return sum;
}

/// The LABEL of the LARGEST selected object by area, ties broken by the earliest label -- the join over the SUBSET, not the scene. May
/// differ from the whole scene's largest object whenever that object lies outside the selection. Refuses `EmptySelection` when the
/// selection holds no object, `BadSelection` for a malformed label set.
pub fn largest_in_selection(manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, selection: []const u32) !u32 {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, manifest.components));
    try validate_selection(manifest, selection);
    if (selection.len == 0) return error.EmptySelection;

    var found: bool = false;
    var best_label: u32 = 0;
    var best_area: u32 = 0;
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < selection.len) : (i += 1) {
        const entry = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, selection[i]);
        // strictly greater keeps the earliest label on an area tie -- the selection is ascending, so the earliest label wins.
        if (!found or entry.area > best_area) {
            found = true;
            best_area = entry.area;
            best_label = entry.label;
        }
    }
    // invariant: a non-empty selection always names a largest object.
    assert(found);
    // invariant: the chosen label is a real, dense label within the selection's own range.
    assert(best_label >= 1);
    assert(best_label <= manifest.components);
    return best_label;
}

/// HOW MANY selected objects enclose at least one hole -- the filter over the SUBSET. Strictly sharper than the scene's holed count,
/// since a holed object outside the selection does not count. Refuses `BadSelection` for a malformed label set.
pub fn holed_in_selection_count(manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, selection: []const u32) !u32 {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, manifest.components));
    try validate_selection(manifest, selection);

    var holed: u32 = 0;
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < selection.len) : (i += 1) {
        const entry = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, selection[i]);
        if (entry.holes >= 1) holed += 1;
    }
    // invariant: no more objects can be holed than the selection holds.
    assert(holed <= @as(u32, @intCast(selection.len)));
    return holed;
}

/// The LABEL of the LARGEST selected object that encloses at least one hole -- the read that joins area AND holes over the SUBSET,
/// ties broken by the earliest label. May differ from the scene's `largest_holed` whenever that object lies outside the selection.
/// Refuses `NoHoledInSelection` by name when no selected object is holed (a fact about holes, not emptiness), `BadSelection` for a
/// malformed label set.
pub fn largest_holed_in_selection(manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, selection: []const u32) !u32 {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, manifest.components));
    try validate_selection(manifest, selection);

    var found: bool = false;
    var best_label: u32 = 0;
    var best_area: u32 = 0;
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < selection.len) : (i += 1) {
        const entry = try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, selection[i]);
        if (entry.holes >= 1) {
            if (!found or entry.area > best_area) {
                found = true;
                best_area = entry.area;
                best_label = entry.label;
            }
        }
    }
    if (!found) return error.NoHoledInSelection;
    // invariant: the chosen label is a real, dense label within the selection's own range.
    assert(best_label >= 1);
    assert(best_label <= manifest.components);
    return best_label;
}

/// The selection SORTED by area DESCENDING, ties broken by ascending label -- a fresh label slice a keeper reads largest-first. Since
/// a selection arrives sorted by label, this is the only read that can reorder it by size. Returns a valid empty slice for an empty
/// selection. Refuses `BadSelection` for a malformed label set. The caller owns the returned slice.
pub fn sort_selection_by_area(allocator: std.mem.Allocator, manifest: scene_manifest.Manifest, selection: []const u32) ![]u32 {
    // invariant: the manifest holds exactly one row per component.
    assert(manifest.objects.len == @as(usize, manifest.components));
    try validate_selection(manifest, selection);

    const out = try allocator.alloc(u32, selection.len);
    var k: usize = 0;
    while (k < out.len) : (k += 1) out[k] = selection[k];

    // insertion sort by (area descending, label ascending) -- bounded by the selection length, itself under the pixel ceiling. Each
    // comparison is one manifest lookup; the selection was validated, so every lookup names a real object.
    var i: usize = 1;
    while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
        const key = out[i];
        const key_area = (try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, key)).area;
        var j: usize = i;
        while (j > 0) {
            const prior = out[j - 1];
            const prior_area = (try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, prior)).area;
            // the key comes before the prior when it is strictly larger by area, or equal-area with a smaller label.
            const key_before = key_area > prior_area or (key_area == prior_area and key < prior);
            if (!key_before) break;
            out[j] = prior;
            j -= 1;
        }
        out[j] = key;
    }

    // invariant: the sorted slice is exactly as long as the selection -- a permutation, nothing added or dropped.
    assert(out.len == selection.len);
    // invariant: the result is non-increasing by area -- the sort postcondition, checked over the whole slice.
    var v: usize = 1;
    while (v < out.len) : (v += 1) {
        const a = (try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, out[v - 1])).area;
        const b = (try scene_manifest.object_of(manifest, out[v])).area;
        assert(a >= b);
    }
    return out;
}

// --- selftest: the DECISIVE subset crux (a selection whose largest and largest-holed both differ from the whole scene's; a filter
//     sharper than the scene's holed count; a non-empty unholed selection refusing NoHoledInSelection; a sort that reverses the label
//     order); the whole selection agrees with the scene's aggregates; an empty selection; malformed selections refusing BadSelection;
//     determinism -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Mint a one-color RGBA canvas -- a local, bounded builder so this rung leans on no higher module.
fn make_canvas(garden: std.mem.Allocator, w: u32, h: u32, fill: u8) !qoi.Pixmap {
    // invariant: a canvas has real extent on both axes.
    assert(w >= 1);
    assert(h >= 1);
    const total: u64 = @as(u64, w) * @as(u64, h);
    // invariant: the canvas stays within the image module's named pixel ceiling.
    assert(total <= qoi.qoi_max_pixels);
    const pixels = try garden.alloc(u8, @as(usize, @intCast(total)) * 4);
    var i: usize = 0;
    while (i < pixels.len) : (i += 4) {
        pixels[i] = fill;
        pixels[i + 1] = fill;
        pixels[i + 2] = fill;
        pixels[i + 3] = 255;
    }
    return qoi.Pixmap{ .width = w, .height = h, .channels = 4, .colorspace = 0, .pixels = pixels };
}

/// Set one foreground pixel at (x, y).
fn set_dot(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32) void {
    // invariant: the write stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x < img.width);
    assert(y < img.height);
    const di = (@as(usize, y) * @as(usize, img.width) + @as(usize, x)) * 4;
    img.pixels[di] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 1] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 2] = 255;
    img.pixels[di + 3] = 255;
}

/// Set one pixel back to background at (x, y) -- carve a hole in a filled shape.
fn clear_dot(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32) void {
    // invariant: the write stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x < img.width);
    assert(y < img.height);
    const di = (@as(usize, y) * @as(usize, img.width) + @as(usize, x)) * 4;
    img.pixels[di] = 0;
    img.pixels[di + 1] = 0;
    img.pixels[di + 2] = 0;
    img.pixels[di + 3] = 255;
}

/// Paint a solid `bw` x `bh` foreground block with its top-left corner at (x, y).
fn set_block(img: *qoi.Pixmap, x: u32, y: u32, bw: u32, bh: u32) void {
    // invariant: the block stays inside the buffer on both axes.
    assert(x + bw <= img.width);
    assert(y + bh <= img.height);
    var yy: u32 = y;
    while (yy < y + bh) : (yy += 1) {
        var xx: u32 = x;
        while (xx < x + bw) : (xx += 1) set_dot(img, xx, yy);
    }
}

fn run_selftest(garden: std.mem.Allocator) !u8 {
    // --- THE CRUX SCENE -- the same four objects image/scene_manifest.rye proves its join over, stacked with a blank row between each
    //     so they are distinct 8-connected components in dense label order: (1) a solid 5x5 block -- area 25, no hole; (2) a 3x3 ring --
    //     area 8, one hole; (3) a 5x5 ring -- area 16, one hole; (4) a solid 2x2 block -- area 4, no hole. Width 7 ---------------------
    var scene_img = try make_canvas(garden, 7, 20, 0);
    set_block(&scene_img, 1, 1, 5, 5); // object 1: solid block, area 25, no hole
    set_block(&scene_img, 1, 7, 3, 3); // object 2: 3x3 ring...
    clear_dot(&scene_img, 2, 8); // ...hole carved, area 8, one hole
    set_block(&scene_img, 1, 11, 5, 5); // object 3: 5x5 ring...
    var ry: u32 = 12;
    while (ry <= 14) : (ry += 1) {
        var rx: u32 = 2;
        while (rx <= 4) : (rx += 1) clear_dot(&scene_img, rx, ry); // ...inner 3x3 carved, area 16, one hole
    }
    set_block(&scene_img, 1, 17, 2, 2); // object 4: solid 2x2 block, area 4, no hole

    const map = try label.label_components(garden, &scene_img);
    if (map.count != 4) return error.SceneNotFourComponents;
    const manifest = try scene_manifest.build_manifest(garden, &map);
    if (scene_manifest.object_count(manifest) != 4) return error.ManifestNotFourObjects;

    // the whole-scene anchors this rung's reads are proven strictly sharper than: the scene's LARGEST object is the solid block
    // (label 1, area 25) and its LARGEST HOLED is the large ring (label 3, area 16); the scene holds two holed objects
    if (try scene_manifest.largest_holed(manifest) != 3) return error.SceneLargestHoledNotLargeRing;
    if (scene_manifest.holed_object_count(manifest) != 2) return error.SceneHoledCountNotTwo;
    if (scene_manifest.total_area(manifest) != 53) return error.SceneTotalAreaNotFiftyThree;

    // --- THE SUBSET MOVES THE ANSWER -- the selection {small ring (2), small block (4)} EXCLUDES both the scene's largest object
    //     (the solid block, label 1) and the scene's largest holed (the large ring, label 3). largest_in_selection names the SMALL
    //     RING (area 8 > 4), largest_holed_in_selection names the SMALL RING too -- neither lands where the whole-scene reads do -----
    const sub = [_]u32{ 2, 4 };
    if (try total_selected_area(manifest, &sub) != 12) return error.SubTotalAreaNotTwelve; // 8 + 4, not the scene's 53
    if (try largest_in_selection(manifest, &sub) != 2) return error.SubLargestNotSmallRing; // not label 1, the scene largest
    if (try largest_holed_in_selection(manifest, &sub) != 2) return error.SubLargestHoledNotSmallRing; // not label 3
    // --- THE FILTER IS SHARPER -- the selection holds ONE holed object though the scene holds two: the large ring is holed yet
    //     unselected, so it does not count ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (try holed_in_selection_count(manifest, &sub) != 1) return error.SubHoledCountNotOne;

    // --- A NON-EMPTY UNHOLED SELECTION -- {solid block (1), small block (4)}: both unholed, so largest_holed_in_selection refuses
    //     NoHoledInSelection even though the selection holds objects -- the refusal is about holes, not emptiness --------------------
    const unholed = [_]u32{ 1, 4 };
    if (try holed_in_selection_count(manifest, &unholed) != 0) return error.UnholedCountNotZero;
    if (largest_holed_in_selection(manifest, &unholed)) |_| return error.UnholedLargestHoledNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.NoHoledInSelection) return error.WrongUnholedError;
    }
    // yet largest_in_selection still answers -- the solid block (area 25 > 4)
    if (try largest_in_selection(manifest, &unholed) != 1) return error.UnholedLargestNotBlock;

    // --- THE SORT REVERSES THE LABEL ORDER -- the selection {small ring (2), large ring (3)} arrives ascending by label; sorted by
    //     area DESCENDING it returns the LARGE RING first (area 16 > 8), labels 3 then 2 -- the reversal only the manifest join reads
    const rings = [_]u32{ 2, 3 };
    const sorted_rings = try sort_selection_by_area(garden, manifest, &rings);
    if (sorted_rings.len != 2) return error.SortedRingsNotTwo;
    if (sorted_rings[0] != 3 or sorted_rings[1] != 2) return error.SortedRingsNotReversed;

    // --- THE WHOLE SELECTION AGREES WITH THE SCENE -- {1,2,3,4} sums to the scene's total area, its largest is the solid block, its
    //     sort is area-descending [1,3,2,4] (25,16,8,4), and its holed count matches the scene's two ---------------------------------
    const whole = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 3, 4 };
    if (try total_selected_area(manifest, &whole) != scene_manifest.total_area(manifest)) return error.WholeAreaNotScene;
    if (try largest_in_selection(manifest, &whole) != 1) return error.WholeLargestNotBlock;
    if (try holed_in_selection_count(manifest, &whole) != 2) return error.WholeHoledCountNotTwo;
    if (try largest_holed_in_selection(manifest, &whole) != 3) return error.WholeLargestHoledNotLargeRing;
    const sorted_whole = try sort_selection_by_area(garden, manifest, &whole);
    if (sorted_whole.len != 4) return error.SortedWholeNotFour;
    if (sorted_whole[0] != 1 or sorted_whole[1] != 3 or sorted_whole[2] != 2 or sorted_whole[3] != 4) return error.SortedWholeNotByArea;

    // --- AN EMPTY SELECTION -- valid: zero area, zero holed, an empty sort; largest_in_selection refuses EmptySelection and
    //     largest_holed_in_selection refuses NoHoledInSelection -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    const empty = [_]u32{};
    if (try total_selected_area(manifest, &empty) != 0) return error.EmptyAreaNotZero;
    if (try holed_in_selection_count(manifest, &empty) != 0) return error.EmptyHoledCountNotZero;
    const sorted_empty = try sort_selection_by_area(garden, manifest, &empty);
    if (sorted_empty.len != 0) return error.EmptySortNotEmpty;
    if (largest_in_selection(manifest, &empty)) |_| return error.EmptyLargestNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.EmptySelection) return error.WrongEmptyError;
    }
    if (largest_holed_in_selection(manifest, &empty)) |_| return error.EmptyLargestHoledNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.NoHoledInSelection) return error.WrongEmptyHoledError;
    }

    // --- MALFORMED SELECTIONS -- a label past the count, background 0, a non-ascending pair, and a too-long slice all refuse
    //     BadSelection, having read nothing --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    const past = [_]u32{5};
    if (total_selected_area(manifest, &past)) |_| return error.PastLabelNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadSelection) return error.WrongPastError;
    }
    const zero = [_]u32{0};
    if (largest_in_selection(manifest, &zero)) |_| return error.ZeroLabelNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadSelection) return error.WrongZeroError;
    }
    const descending = [_]u32{ 3, 2 };
    if (holed_in_selection_count(manifest, &descending)) |_| return error.DescendingNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadSelection) return error.WrongDescendingError;
    }
    const dup = [_]u32{ 2, 2 };
    if (sort_selection_by_area(garden, manifest, &dup)) |_| return error.DupNotRefused else |err| {
        if (err != error.BadSelection) return error.WrongDupError;
    }

    // --- DETERMINISM -- the same selection reads the same summary twice --------------------------------------------------------
    if (try largest_in_selection(manifest, &sub) != try largest_in_selection(manifest, &sub)) return error.SummaryNotDeterministic;
    const s1 = try sort_selection_by_area(garden, manifest, &whole);
    const s2 = try sort_selection_by_area(garden, manifest, &whole);
    var d: usize = 0;
    while (d < s1.len) : (d += 1) {
        if (s1[d] != s2[d]) return error.SummaryNotDeterministic;
    }

    print("GREEN image-selection-summary: read a SELECTION as a small table -- JOIN each selected object's scene-manifest facts (area, holes) over the SUBSET a selector named, exact integers, NO float and NO tolerance -- the DECISIVE subset crux, strictly sharper than the whole-scene reads: over a scene of a big solid block (area 25, no hole), a small 3x3 ring (area 8, one hole), a large 5x5 ring (area 16, one hole), and a small 2x2 block (area 4, no hole), the selection {{small ring, small block}} EXCLUDES both the scene's largest object (the solid block) and the scene's largest holed (the large ring), so largest_in_selection names the SMALL RING (area 8) and largest_holed_in_selection names the SMALL RING too, where neither whole-scene read lands; the filter is sharper than the scene's -- that selection holds ONE holed object though the scene holds two, the large ring holed yet unselected not counting, and a non-empty selection of only unholed objects refuses NoHoledInSelection (a fact about holes, not emptiness); the sort reverses the label order -- the selection {{small ring, large ring}}, ascending by label 2 then 3, sorts by area descending to the LARGE RING first, labels 3 then 2; the whole selection agrees with the scene -- total_selected_area sums to the scene's 53, the largest is the solid block, the sort is area-descending [1,3,2,4], the holed count matches two; an empty selection is valid (zero area, empty sort) yet largest_in_selection refuses EmptySelection; a label past the count, background 0, a non-ascending pair, and a duplicate all refuse BadSelection having read nothing; the same selection reads the same summary twice. Compose not braid over image/scene_manifest.rye (the per-object rows) and the selection ladder (hit_test, region_select, lasso_select) that speaks the label set\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    const garden = init.arena.allocator();
    const args = try init.minimal.args.toSlice(garden);
    if (args.len >= 2 and std.mem.eql(u8, args[1], "selftest")) {
        return run_selftest(garden);
    }
    print("usage: selection_summary selftest\n", .{});
    return 1;
}
